{
  "0": "Interaction mechanism: replace dot-product compatibility with diagonal bilinear scoring s(h,i)=sum_j[(1+0.1*tanh(a_j))*h_j*e_ij], initializing a=0 and learning it jointly. This starts at the existing scorer and permits modest dimension-specific relevance without a costly interaction network. Preserve GRU topology, features, history readout, loss and optimizer. Apply to all four categories, with one independently trained checkpoint per category; update the predictor writer to reproduce scoring.",
  "1": "Interest aggregation: use h_read=(1-g)*h_last+g*h_previous, where g=0.1*sigmoid(a) is a jointly learned scalar initialized with a=0. For one valid state, use that state alone. This bounded readout may reduce sensitivity to a noisy final interaction while retaining recent intent, with negligible cost. Use causal prefix readouts wherever training requires them. Preserve history selection, embeddings, GRU topology, scoring, loss and optimizer across all four category models; update the predictor writer.",
  "2": "Training strategy: retain the existing optimizer and initial learning rate, but apply cosine learning-rate decay over the existing 200-epoch cap, reaching 10% of the initial rate at epoch 200. Gradual decay may stabilize later ranking improvements without increasing updates per epoch. Preserve batch size, dropout, weight decay, early stopping, loss and all model components. Keep preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, fixed evaluation and resource allocation, with one selected checkpoint for each of the four categories.",
  "3": "Embedding representation: normalize each nonpadding input item embedding by its RMS, e'=e/sqrt(mean(e^2)+1e-6), immediately before the existing input processing. Keep padding zero and leave candidate scoring embeddings unchanged. Controlling input magnitude may reduce GRU gate saturation and improve sparse-item generalization. Preserve embedding dimensions and tying, features, GRU topology, history policy, readout, scorer, loss and optimizer. Apply across all four category models and reproduce normalization in the predictor writer."
}
